### How does a bound instance-method reference work?

The receiver is captured when the reference is created. Only the method's arguments come from the stream.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.List;

public class BoundRefDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> names = List.of("Rajesh", "Priya", "Amit");

        // Lambda
        names.forEach(name -> System.out.println(name));

        // Bound method reference (equivalent)
        names.forEach(System.out::println);
    }
}
```

```text
Rajesh
Priya
Amit
```

### How is bound different from unbound?

| Type | Syntax | Receiver | Stream element provides |
|---|---|---|---|
| Bound | System.out::println | Fixed (System.out) | Method argument |
| Unbound | String::length | Supplied per call | Is the receiver |

```java
// Bound: System.out is the receiver, element is the argument
names.forEach(System.out::println);
// Each call: System.out.println("Rajesh"), System.out.println("Priya"), ...

// Unbound: element IS the receiver
names.stream().map(String::toUpperCase);
// Each call: "Rajesh".toUpperCase(), "Priya".toUpperCase(), ...
```

### Why is System.out::println wrong for production?

System.out writes directly to the console with no log levels, no formatting, and no way to disable it.

```java
// WRONG: production code
accounts.forEach(System.out::println);

// CORRECT: use a Logger
import java.util.logging.Logger;
Logger log = Logger.getLogger("com.bank.hr");
accounts.forEach(a -> log.info(a.toString()));

// Or with a bound reference on the logger:
accounts.stream()
    .map(Object::toString)
    .forEach(log::info);
```

### Can you use bound references with other objects?

Yes. Any object's method can be captured.

```java
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
names.forEach(sb::append);  // appends each name to the same builder
System.out.println(sb);  // RajeshPriyaAmit
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: assuming System.out::println works with formatted output.

```java
// System.out::println prints each element on its own line
// For formatted output, use a lambda:
names.forEach(n -> System.out.printf("Name: %s%n", n));
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- The bound reference captures the receiver once at creation time and reuses it for every call.
- If the receiver changes between creation and use, you get the object as it was at capture time.
- System.out::println is the most common debugging pattern but should never appear in committed code.
- Bound references apply broadly: logger::info, list::add, builder::append.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What is the difference between System.out::println and x -> System.out.println(x)?" Functionally identical; the reference is more concise.
- "Can you capture a method reference to a local variable?" Yes, as long as the variable is effectively final.
- "What happens if System.out is reassigned?" The reference captured the original object; the reassignment has no effect on the reference.
